Let $v(x)=10^{x}$, then $10^{u(x)}=v\Bigl(u(x)\Bigr)$, or $(v\circ u)(x)$.

Therefore, the derivative of $10^{u(x)}$ can be found using **the chain rule**:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{d}{dx}10^{u(x)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[v\Bigl(u(x)\Bigr)\right]&&\gray{\text{Let }v(x)=10^{x}}
\\\\
&=v'\Bigl(u(x)\Bigr)\cdot u'(x)&&\gray{\text{The chain rule}}
\\\\
&=\ln(10)\cdot 10^{u(x)}\cdot u'(x)&&\gray{\dfrac{d}{dx}10^{x}=\ln(10)\cdot 10^{x}}
\end{align}$
